*ātman* (the self) is defined by belonging to the universal *ātmatva*. Because it is extremely subtle, it is not directly perceived; it is inferred through the cognising organs (the ear and the rest), which themselves are inferred from perceptions of *śabda* and the other sense-data. Tools like the axe never act on their own — there is always an agent — and by the same pattern of reasoning the cogniser behind cognition is inferred. Prashastapāda then rules out three candidates for that cogniser:

- not the body, since the body is a material product like a jar and corpses have no consciousness;
- not the sense-organs, since these are mere instruments, and we remember objects after the organ is damaged or absent;
- not the *manas*, since if it functioned without other organs perception and memory would arise simultaneously, and *manas* itself is only an instrument.

By elimination (*pariśeṣa*), consciousness is the work of *ātman*, which is therefore known through this consciousness. Like inferring a charioteer from the chariot's purposive movement, the agent ruling the body is inferred from its purposeful activity and cessation. The agent is further inferred through six concrete signs: (1) the regulated working of the breath-air (like a blower at the bellows), (2) the rhythmic blinking (like the operator of a wooden machine), (3) the healing of wounds and broken parts (like a house-owner repairing his house), (4) the directed movement of *manas* toward the sense-organ contacting a desired object (like a boy bouncing a ball in a corner of the room), (5) salivation following the mere sight of a remembered taste (one viewer looking through two windows — one self witnessing both eye-cognition and tongue-modification), and (6) the qualities *sukha*, *duḥkha*, *icchā*, *dveṣa*, *prayatna* — which cannot be qualities of the body or sense-organs (they don't track the 'I'-notion, occur only in certain parts, don't last as long as the substrate, and aren't perceptible to outer organs) and which are signified by 'I' — a word wholly different from 'earth', 'water', etc.

The *guṇa* of *ātman* are fourteen: *buddhi*, *sukha*, *duḥkha*, *icchā*, *dveṣa*, *prayatna*, *dharma*, *adharma*, *saṃskāra*, Number, Dimension, Separateness, *saṃyoga*, Disjunction. Each is anchored to a sutra. Notably, *dharma/adharma* are inferred from the fact that one self's qualities don't cause qualities in another self; *saṃskāra* is inferred as cause of memory; the self's dimension is *paramamahat* (highest); and the contact-born character of pleasure-and-the-rest proves that *saṃyoga* exists in the self, with disjunction as its destroyer.

*manas* (mind) is so called by belonging to the universal *manastva*. Even when *ātman*, *indriya*, and the object are all in proximity, *jñāna*, *sukha*, and the rest do not always appear; this gap forces us to infer another instrument. Two further facts narrow it down: memories arise even when the outer organs aren't operating, and the pleasures, pains, etc. that this instrument grasps aren't reachable by any outer organ. Hence the inferred instrument is *antaḥkaraṇa* — internal. Its *guṇa* are eight: Number, Dimension, Separateness, *saṃyoga*, Disjunction, Priority, Posteriority, *saṃskāra* (faculty). Each is anchored to a Vaiśeṣika sūtra:

- non-simultaneity of effort and knowledge implies one *manas* per body, hence its singular Number (and from this, Separateness);
- the sutra noting the absence of largeness in it implies *aṇu-parimāṇa* — atomic dimension;
- 'moving away' and 'moving toward' imply *saṃyoga* and Disjunction;
- corporeality (*mūrtatva*) implies Priority, Posteriority, and *saṃskāra*;
- being intangible implies it cannot produce further substances;
- being active implies it is corporeal;
- if it were *jña* (conscious), the entire body would become the common ground of all experiences — an unwanted consequence — so *manas* must be unconscious;
- being an instrument (*karaṇa*), it is for the sake of another (*ātman*);
- having qualities, it must be a *dravya*;
- because it carries effort and *adṛṣṭa*, it moves quickly.

With this, the *dravya-padārtha* section of Prashastapāda's *bhāṣya* concludes.
